# evidence-cmd: cd plugins/foundry/mcp-server && uv run --quiet python -c "import shutil, tempfile, pathlib; from foundry_mcp.tools.foundry_report import generate_report; src = pathlib.Path(\"tests/fixtures/escalation/finer_boundary_run\"); root = pathlib.Path(tempfile.mkdtemp()); run = root / \"foundry-archive\" / \"halt-note-run\"; shutil.copytree(src, run); result = generate_report(root, run); print(\"generate_report ok:\", result[\"ok\"]); md = (run / \"REPORT.md\").read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"); print(\"ST-008 anywhere in REPORT.md:\", \"ST-008\" in md); [print(line) for line in md.splitlines() if \"terminal state\" in line]"
# evidence-for: NFR-011, AC-047, FR-027, FR-053, OT-041, US-006

generate_report ok: True
ST-008 anywhere in REPORT.md: False
This run did not halt. A halt is a named terminal state reached by a SUCCESSFUL transition (ST-001), so its absence here means the run ended some other way — not that a halt was refused.
HALTED is a named terminal state and is NOT DONE (ST-001 / CT-004): the report is generated and every open defect is named in it. `Reason` is a member of the halt vocabulary and `Reason text` is the lead's own words; a run whose `halted_reason` predates FR-019 carries a free sentence and no member, and that sentence is printed as text rather than guessed onto a member. The co-dispatch rows are the sets the SERVER computed at each Foundry-Tasks call and the lead acted on — read from the handoff ledger, never recomputed here, because a second answer to 'which castings went out together' could disagree with the one that was actually dispatched. A row with an EMPTY set is a set the server computed and found empty — the casting owned its requirements alone — and is a different fact from a run that dispatched nothing, which is different again from `co_dispatch_not_computable`, where the manifest declares no requirement_ids and the question could not be asked (AC-049).
